ip">Sanggyeong, the Upper Capital, was the main capital of the kingdom of Balhae, in the Mudan River valley near modern Ning'an in Heilongjiang, China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sanghyang-siksa-kandang-karesian"><span class="enc-name">Sanghyang Siksa Kandang Karesian</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sanghyang Siksa Kandang Karesian is an Old Sundanese manuscript dated 1518, from the Sunda Kingdom of western Java.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sanghyang-tapak-inscription"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sanghyang-tapak-inscription.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sanghyang Tapak inscription</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions are four stones found near Cibadak in Sukabumi, West Java, dated to 1030 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sangju"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sangju.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sangju</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sangju is a city in North Gyeongsang province, South Korea, on the upper Nakdong river.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sangkum-reastr-niyum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sangkum-reastr-niyum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sangkum Reastr Niyum</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sangkum Reastr Niyum, the People's Socialist Community, was the political movement founded by Norodom Sihanouk in 1955 after he abdicated the throne to enter…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sangpyeong-tongbo"><span class="enc-name">Sangpyeong tongbo</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sangpyeong tongbo was a copper coin of Joseon Korea, cast in large numbers from 1678 under King Sukjong as the country's standard currency.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sangram-shah"><span class="enc-name">Sangram Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sangram Shah was a Gond king of Garha-Katanga in central India in the early 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sangrama-vijayatunggavarman"><span class="enc-name">Sangrama Vijayatunggavarman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sangrama Vijayatunggavarman was a ruler of Srivijaya in the early eleventh century, known from the Tanjore inscription of the Chola king Rajendra Chola I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sangramaraja"><span class="enc-name">Sangramaraja</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sangramaraja was king of Kashmir from 1003 to 1028 and founder of the Lohara dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sangro"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sangro.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sangro</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sangro is a river of central Italy, about 117 km long, rising in the Abruzzo Apennines and flowing into the Adriatic south of Ortona.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sangrur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sangrur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sangrur</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sangrur is a city in southern Punjab, India, and the headquarters of Sangrur district.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sangye-gyatso"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sangye-gyatso.jpg" alt="" loading="